    "title": "Dodgers legend Davey Lopes passes away at 80",
    "text": "Los Angeles Dodgers legend Davey Lopes has passed away. He was 80 years old. The four-time MLB All-Star and two-time World Series champion died “following complications from Parkinson’s disease” at his home in Rhode Island, “surrounded by his family,” TMZ reported. “The Dodgers mourn the loss of Davey Lopes, who passed away today at age 80,” the team posted on Facebook. “Lopes was a member of the team’s record-setting infield of the 1970s and 1980s and one of the finest basestealers in MLB history. Our condolences go out to his family and friends.” Lopes was born and raised in Rhode Island and attended college at Iowa Wesleyan College and Washburn University in Kansas. Los Angeles Dodgers second baseman Davey Lopes smiles and laughs in the club’s locker room in Los Angeles on Oct. 10, 1978, following the team’s 11-5 win over … He was drafted by the San Francisco Giants in 1967, but opted not to sign. He then joined the Dodgers during the second round of the 1968 MLB January Draft. However, his Major League Baseball debut came in 1972 at the age of 27 with the Boys in Blue. Lopes was part of the most iconic infield in Dodgers history. He, along with Steve Garvey, Ron Cey and Bill Russell, helped lead the team to four National League pennants in the ’70s and early ’80s. He was also key in the Dodgers’ 1981 World Series win over the Yankees. The famed base stealer then went on to play for the Oakland A’s from 1982 to 1984. In the Bay Area, his base-stealing prowess alongside teammate Rickey Henderson earned them 158 stolen bases, with Henderson stealing 130 and Lopes stealing 28. The statistic set a new American League record for the duo. He later played for the Chicago Cubs from 1984 to 1986 and the Houston Astros from 1986 to 1987. He retired at the end of the ’87 season with 155 home runs, a .263 batting average, 557 stolen bases and played 1,812 games. While he hung up his cleats, he didn’t completely leave the game. Lopes was a bench coach for the Texas Rangers from 1989 to 1991, coached first base for the Baltimore Orioles from 1992 to 1994 and the San Diego Padres from 1995 to 1999. during the (blank) inning of an exhibition spring training baseball game on Feb. 26, 2013, in Glendale. Ariz. (AP Photo/Marcio Jose Sanchez) He served as manager of the Milwaukee Brewers from 2000 to 2002. He reunited with the Padres to coach first base from 2003 to 2005. It was a job he continued when he moved on to the Washington Nationals in 2006 and the Philadelphia Phillies from 2007 to 2010. During this time, he earned a World Series ring for the second time during the 2008 Fall Classic. He then made his way back to Dodger Stadium to coach first base until 2015. He then ended his career as first base coach for the Washington Nationals from 2015 to 2017. This is a developing story. Check back for updates.",
